## Grey structure

Grey structure — foundation, frame, slabs, blockwork, plaster, conduiting and roof treatment, with no finishes — currently runs PKR 3,200 to 3,900 per square foot of covered area in Lahore for a standard residential build on good ground. Karachi runs slightly higher on steel logistics; Islamabad higher again on aggregate haulage.

Three things move that number more than anything else:

**Steel rate.** Deformed bar has been the single most volatile line item for four years. A PKR 20,000 per tonne swing moves a 1-kanal house by roughly PKR 180 per square foot.

**Soil.** A raft where a strip footing would have done adds eight to twelve per cent to substructure cost. This is exactly why we do not price a job before the borehole report.

**Span.** Every metre of clear span beyond about 5.5 metres costs disproportionately in slab depth and reinforcement.

## Finishing

Finishing is where the range explodes, because it is entirely specification-driven. PKR 2,400 per square foot buys a competent, durable, unremarkable finish. PKR 6,000 buys imported stone, bespoke joinery and premium sanitary ware. Above PKR 9,000 you are in bespoke territory and per-square-foot rates stop being a useful way to think about it.

## Turnkey

For a well-specified residential turnkey job in Lahore, budget PKR 6,200 to 8,500 per square foot all-in. Anyone quoting materially below that is either excluding something or intends to claim it later as a variation.

## The honest advice

Do not select a contractor on rate. Select on the quality of the BOQ. A contractor who hands you a fifteen-page priced BOQ with named brands and quantities has thought about your project. A contractor who hands you a rate per square foot has not.','Nadia Sultan','Cost',6,'2026-07-14','["cost","residential","BOQ"]'::jsonb),
('lda-approval-process-explained','The LDA approval process, explained without the runaround','What you actually need to submit, in what order, and where the delays really come from.','Most people experience LDA approval as an opaque process that takes as long as it takes. It is not opaque; it is just sequential, and almost every delay comes from submitting an incomplete set.

## What you need before you start

Ownership documents (registry or allotment plus transfer letter), a fresh demarcation certificate, the approved site plan for the scheme, and a No Demand Certificate. Missing NDC is the single most common cause of a rejected first submission.

## The drawing set

Site plan, all floor plans, two sections, all elevations, and a structural stability certificate signed by a PEC-registered engineer. Drawings must be to LDA title-block format and the covered-area calculation on the sheet must reconcile exactly with the fee calculation. A mismatch of even a few square feet gets the file returned.

## Timeline

A complete residential submission on a clean title typically clears in six to ten weeks. Commercial takes longer because of the additional fire and parking scrutiny. The clock only starts on a complete file — which is why we run an internal completeness check against a written checklist before anything goes in.

## Where delays actually come from

In our experience: incomplete NDC, covered-area mismatch, demarcation older than six months, and structural certificates signed by an engineer whose PEC registration has lapsed. All four are avoidable in the week before submission.','Nadia Sultan','Regulation',5,'2026-05-22','["LDA","approvals","Lahore"]'::jsonb),
('why-soil-testing-is-not-optional','Why soil testing is not optional, even on a 10-marla plot','The borehole costs less than one per cent of your build. Skipping it has cost clients far more.','We get asked to waive the geotechnical investigation on small residential plots roughly once a month. The argument is always the same: the neighbours built without one and their houses are fine.

Here is the problem with that argument.

## Ground varies over short distances

Lahore alluvial deposits are not uniform. We have recorded a bearing capacity difference of more than forty per cent between two boreholes eleven metres apart on the same plot in Johar Town. Your neighbour ground is not your ground.

## Old fill is invisible from the surface

A substantial proportion of plots in newer sectors sit partly on filled ground — an old watercourse, a borrow pit, a demolished structure. Compacted properly, it is fine. Not compacted, it settles differentially, and differential settlement cracks a building in a way that is expensive and sometimes impossible to fix.

## Water table changes the design

If the water table is within the founding depth, you need dewatering during construction and waterproofing detail afterward. Discovering this at excavation stage rather than at design stage is the difference between a planned cost and an emergency one.

## The economics

Three boreholes to fifteen metres with laboratory classification costs a fraction of one per cent of a typical build. A raft you did not need costs eight to twelve per cent. An underpinning remedy after settlement costs more than either and takes months.

Do the test.','Bilal Cheema','Engineering',4,'2026-03-08','["geotechnical","soil","residential"]'::jsonb),
('passive-cooling-punjab-climate','Designing for 47 degrees: passive cooling that actually works in Punjab','Orientation, mass and shading will cut your cooling load before you spend a rupee on equipment.','Air conditioning is treated as the answer to Punjab summer. It is the last answer, not the first. Four design decisions, made before anything is built, reduce the cooling load substantially — and they cost nothing extra if made early.

## Orientation

The west elevation is the problem. Late-afternoon sun on a west-facing glazed wall in June drives peak cooling load. Where the plot allows, put service spaces — stairs, stores, washrooms — on the west and habitable rooms north and east.

## Thermal mass, correctly placed

Mass on the outside of insulation stores heat and re-radiates it into the room at night, which is the opposite of what you want. Mass on the inside of insulation buffers the daily swing. In practice this means insulating on the outer face of the wall, not the inner.

## Shading over glazing specification

A horizontal fin that keeps summer sun off the glass does more than any glass coating. The geometry is straightforward: size the projection for the June solar altitude at your latitude and it self-adjusts to admit winter sun.

## Courtyards and stack ventilation

The traditional courtyard house works because a shaded courtyard becomes a cool-air reservoir and drives cross ventilation through the plan. It is a genuinely good climatic response and it is why we designed AMARC Courtyard Homes around it rather than treating the courtyard as a decorative gesture.

Do these four things and the mechanical load drops far enough to change the plant size — which pays for itself in equipment cost before it ever touches your electricity bill.','Ayesha Rauf','Design',5,'2026-01-19','["design","climate","sustainability"]'::jsonb)
